WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:02.302
On December 19th, 2018...

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:05.605
a revolution started to spread 
throughout Sudan.

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:10.744
After decades of living under 
President Omar Bashir’s

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.078
brutal military regime...

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:13.880
civilians pushed back.

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:17.817
They wanted a democracy 
in their country.

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:19.486
A few months later,

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:22.922
this man and this man helped take down 
Bashir in a coup...

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:26.659
and then promised protesters the future 
they had demanded.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.762
Four years later

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:31.164
the same two men are now 
at war with each other...

00:00:31.464 --> 00:00:34.534
tearing Sudan apart, killing 
hundreds of civilians.

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:37.437
So how did Sudan go from this...

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:39.873
to this in such a short time?

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.842
And how did these 
two powerful men...

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:46.046
go from partners to enemies?

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:49.349
[overlapping] -Clashes in Sudan.
-...a deadly coup...

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:50.683
... support forces...

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:54.788
Sudan has a long history of coups.

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:59.259
Leader after leader has been brought down 
by military officers.

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.361
The country's official military

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:04.597
now known as the Sudanese 
Armed Forces or SAF

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:07.967
has held tremendous power in the country 
for nearly a century.

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:09.936
And they started using that power

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:13.406
soon after Sudan gained independence 
from Anglo Egyptian rule.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:17.010
“Sudanese flag now replacing 
the flags of those two nations.”

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:20.580
The first successful military coup 
happened in 1958.

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.783
Abdullah Kalil, a retired 
military officer

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:25.118
and sitting prime minister...

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:27.487
overthrew his own civilian government

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.589
to put Sudan under military rule.

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:32.659
About a decade later, Colonel 
Jaafar Nimeiry

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.461
carried out another successful coup...

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.996
bringing down a short lived democracy.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:39.799
Then in 1985, Nimeiry was out.

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:42.402
This military officer took him down

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:44.871
and later installed a new 
democratic government.

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:48.108
Four years later, Colonel 
Omar Bashir took down

00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:51.044
this government and appointed himself 
as the new head of state.

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.580
But Bashir ended up being different

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.914
from those that came before him.

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:57.884
Given the pattern of military takeovers...

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:01.421
Bashir knew he may suffer 
the same fate as previous leaders.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:04.457
So he used a strategy called 
coup proofing...

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.726
where he'd surround himself 
with protectors 

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:10.263
but would keep each one in check 
so they couldn't overthrow him.

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.332
It started with the SAF.

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:15.168
Throughout his regime, Bashir 
bolstered the army

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:17.137
and maintained a strong relationship with them.

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:19.839
He relied on them heavily 
to crack down

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:22.142
on an ongoing civil war 
in southern Sudan...

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:24.110
where SAF and allied militias

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.312
brutalized civilians on his command.

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.848
Then, with the army busy 
in the south

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.251
another war started taking shape 
in the West.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:34.220
Darfur was in crisis.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.357
People here were historically neglected.

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:39.726
Lack of medical supplies 
and necessary goods

00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:42.462
and little representation 
in Sudan's government

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:44.631
left them feeling agitated for years.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:46.900
So in 2003

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.502
while large portions of the army 
were tied up in the South.

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:52.472
Rebel groups attacked troops 
in this city in Darfur.

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:57.210
And Bashir in recognizing that 
this rebellion was taking place

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.612
instead of relying on the Sudan Armed Forces

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.548
or SAF, the conventional military

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:04.617
he instead decided to 
rely and arm 

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:07.120
local Arab militias in the region

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.821
that were known as the Janjaweed.

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:10.623
This group was brutal

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:13.760
and focused on wiping out 
Darfuri rebels and civilians

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.662
at Bashir’s direction.

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:18.932
Satellite imagery shows that the Janjaweed
is likely responsible

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:21.067
for destroying over 3000 villages.

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:26.139
SAF troops in Darfur were responsible 
for destruction there as well.

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:29.442
Both groups are accused 
of mass killing, rape

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:31.744
and the targeted displacement of civilians.

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:35.882
Together, they killed thousands 
of Darfuris.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.119
The events in Darfur showed 
Bashir how to keep his power.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.723
And he turned to the Janjaweed 
in search of another protector.

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:47.360
Among the Janjaweed militias

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.262
there were a couple of men 
Bashir trusted

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.264
and tried to bring into 
the central government.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.167
But there is one particular
Janjaweed leader

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:55.568
that Bashir trusted the most.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:59.072
Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo  or Hemeti.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.275
Bashir called him “my protection”.

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:05.178
A particular play on the Arabic word Hemmati

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:08.381
which is “my protection” versus Hemeti, 
which is his nickname.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:12.518
By 2011, the long and gruesome 
civil war here ended

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:14.354
with South Sudan 
gaining independence...

00:04:14.621 --> 00:04:16.789
leaving Bashir in a weak position.

00:04:17.624 --> 00:04:20.159
And soon after, Sudan's economy tanked.

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:23.363
Many of the oil resources 
that sustain the country

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:24.631
were based in the South

00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:26.799
which was no longer 
under Bashir's control.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.701
To strengthen his power

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.638
Bashir gave Hemeti and the 
Janjaweed official status

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:37.010
as a paramilitary force called the 
Rapid Support Forces, or RSF, in 2013.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.413
At first, the group was placed under the NISS

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:44.150
Sudan's intelligence agency, 
also one of Bashir's protectors.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.119
Even though they were supporting the SAF

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:47.754
in the ongoing war in Darfur.

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:51.391
Then in 2017, he passed a law

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:53.593
placing Hemeti directly 
under his command...

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:57.630
making it clear that the RSF’s main purpose 
was to protect him.

00:04:58.531 --> 00:05:00.466
To maintain the RSF’s loyalty

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:03.536
Bashir gave Hemeti financial autonomy 
and allowed him

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:05.738
to take control of some of 
Darfur's gold mines...

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:09.542
smuggle weapons and minerals 
into places like Chad and Libya...

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:13.379
and send troops into war torn regions 
in exchange for money.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.848
At the same time, Bashir continued

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:18.217
to let the SAF have a hand 
in major industries...

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:21.120
like weapon production and telecommunications.

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:24.490
While Bashir was busy making 
these two forces richer

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.492
civilians continue to struggle.

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:28.561
Protests broke out in 2018

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:31.230
in the middle of a really bad 
economic crisis.

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:33.933
That's when Bashir faced his biggest challenge...

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.703
putting his protection scheme to the test.

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:40.373
The ultimate trigger was 
the government's budget...

00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:45.211
which allocated about 60 to 70% 
to the security sector...

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:47.580
which included Bashir's protectors.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.550
While Sudanese people struggled 
for basic needs.

00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:53.586
This led to the biggest revolution

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:54.887
in Sudan's history.

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:01.160
Spontaneous protests broke

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.695
out in different parts of the country.

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:06.866
And then they shifted to Khartoum 
in the capital city.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.168
Bashir refused to leave office.

00:06:09.268 --> 00:06:12.305
The RSF, SAF, and other security sectors 
backed him...

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:14.741
and cracked down on the protesters

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:16.776
who ultimately wanted democracy.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.613
But protesters didn't back down for months...

00:06:21.247 --> 00:06:23.616
and it became clear to the RSF and SAF...

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:27.420
that Bashir's leadership wouldn't be 
as useful to them anymore.

00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:30.990
So on April 11th, 2019...

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.460
they made a move that surprised civilians 
and Bashir.

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:38.464
SAF commanders colluded with 
the RSF’s Hemeti 

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:40.333
and removed Bashir from power.

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:46.139
Protesters celebrated Bashir's removal...

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.608
but they didn't trust the man 
who made it happen.

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:52.378
A day after the coup, Abdel Fattah al-Burhan...

00:06:52.612 --> 00:06:54.781
a formal regional commander in Darfur

00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:56.182
took charge of the South.

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:59.519
Together, the two men gained control of Sudan.

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:04.257
People saw them as complicit 
in the violence in Darfur

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:06.192
all those years ago, where they 
worked together.

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:08.494
One as a member of the Sudan Armed Forces.

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:12.298
One as a militia leader on the same side...

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:15.601
against large proportions of the population of Darfur.

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:19.405
And soon, the two men turned on protesters.

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:25.111
When pro-democracy protests 
kept intensifying in Khartoum...

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:28.114
Hemeti and his forces
started cracking down.

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.183
On June 3rd, 2019...

00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:33.953
the RSF killed over 100 people 
at a sit in protest.

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:37.056
And other massacres continued 
throughout the country.

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:38.825
After this...

00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:41.494
these countries were forced to step in

00:07:41.494 --> 00:07:43.863
to help put Sudan on a democratic path.

00:07:44.564 --> 00:07:46.532
The United States, 
with its Arab allies

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:49.202
the United Kingdom, Ethiopia,
and the African Union...

00:07:49.702 --> 00:07:51.871
pressured Sudan's military and protesters...

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:54.006
to accept a power sharing agreement.

00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:55.208
In this new deal

00:07:55.208 --> 00:07:58.044
representatives from both 
the military and protesters...

00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:00.413
would be part of a transitional council.

00:08:01.113 --> 00:08:04.650
In this plan, the military would have control
for 21 months

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.118
and civilians for 18.

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:09.655
That meant the military would eventually 
have to hand over power

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:11.424
to civilians who run the country.

00:08:11.891 --> 00:08:14.994
The problem was, despite 
warnings from protesters...

00:08:15.261 --> 00:08:18.297
these two military men were put 
in charge of the council

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:21.200
with Burhan as chair and 
Hemeti as vice chair.

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:24.237
At first, the council acted in line 
with the agreement...

00:08:24.504 --> 00:08:27.440
and installed a new prime minister, 
Abdallah Hamdok.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.077
But after multiple military interventions 
by these leaders...

00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:33.913
like a staged coup in October 2021...

00:08:34.347 --> 00:08:36.716
Hamdok resigned in January 2022.

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:39.919
That made Burhan, the de facto leader of Sudan...

00:08:40.286 --> 00:08:42.755
and Hemeti as his number two again.

00:08:44.423 --> 00:08:46.292
But Hemeti was never quite comfortable

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:47.894
with playing second fiddle.

00:08:47.894 --> 00:08:49.996
Especially because he had 
amassed this fortune

00:08:49.996 --> 00:08:53.366
and had positioned himself 
to play the role of statesman...

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:55.902
almost better than Burhan himself.

00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:59.238
As Burhan developed personal alliances 
with leaders

00:08:59.238 --> 00:09:01.874
in the Middle East, Europe, 
and Asia over the years.

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:05.611
Hemeti did the same using his riches 
from the gold mines...

00:09:05.945 --> 00:09:08.648
to build relationships with powerful individuals

00:09:08.648 --> 00:09:10.483
in those countries as well.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.585
After another year of protests...

00:09:12.919 --> 00:09:16.355
the US, UAE, Saudi Arabia, 
and the UK

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:20.226
pressured Burhan, Hemeti, and 
protesters to sign another deal.

00:09:20.526 --> 00:09:22.895
On December 5th, 2020

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:25.698
Burhan, Hemeti and political parties 
signed it...

00:09:25.965 --> 00:09:30.436
promising a new civilian-led 
transitional government by April 2023.

00:09:30.937 --> 00:09:34.240
But these two men disagreed 
on a key part of the deal.

00:09:35.041 --> 00:09:37.910
The RSF would need to become 
part of Burhan’s army...

00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:40.646
which would limit Hemeti’s power.

00:09:40.646 --> 00:09:42.748
Burhan wanted that to happen
in two years.

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:44.984
But Hemeti proposed 10.

00:09:44.984 --> 00:09:49.188
That disagreement caused a big rift
between Burhan and Hemeti...

00:09:49.555 --> 00:09:52.458
which led to the current conflict between the two men.

00:09:53.626 --> 00:09:57.063
They've placed hundreds of thousands 
of armed men across the country.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.465
Burhan and his army have an air force

00:09:59.465 --> 00:10:01.734
that's responsible for many of the casualties.

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:06.138
And Hemeti’s finances allow him to arm 
more and more men on the ground.

00:10:06.572 --> 00:10:08.874
Hundreds of civilians have been killed already...

00:10:09.241 --> 00:10:11.611
and thousands have fled Sudan.

00:10:11.611 --> 00:10:13.913
Cease fires have routinely been broken.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:17.316
And talks between the warring parties 
have gone nowhere.

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:19.952
What was once a hopeful revolution

00:10:19.952 --> 00:10:22.054
has been interrupted by these two men.

00:10:22.622 --> 00:10:24.156
No matter who wins this war

00:10:24.156 --> 00:10:26.258
protesters are left feeling betrayed...

00:10:26.592 --> 00:10:28.761
by the country's de facto leaders

00:10:28.761 --> 00:10:31.530
and also by the international community

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.633
that claimed to support their hopes

00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:34.934
for democracy.

